      "target": "../sections/sec-000-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Libraries are the heart of every society and a cor-\nnerstone of education, serving as repositories of\nhuman knowledge and cultural heritage. As infor-\nmation landscapes evolve, these institutions must\nadapt to the growing volume and complexity of\ndigital resources. Therefore, technological innova-\ntion in both traditional libraries and modern digital\nlibrary systems is essential to optimize workflows,\nenhance accessibility, and improve resource orga-\nnization. With the rapid advancement of artificial\nintelligence (AI), particularly through Large Lan-\nguage Models (LLMs) (Chang et al., 2024), t",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduction. Libraries need technology to handle growing digital collections, and large language models (LLMs) can help with subject tagging, metadata enrichment and semantic search, and with interoperability with controlled vocabularies such as the GND, though interpretability, tagging bias and multilingual processing remain challenges. LLMs4Subjects, the SemEval-2025 shared task, asks for LLM-based subject tagging of bilingual technical records from Leibniz University's Technical Library (TIBKAT) using the GND taxonomy, in two tasks: learning the GND taxonomy (Task 1) and recommending GND subjects for a record from its title and abstract (Task 2). The authors adapt OntoAligner, a Python ontology-alignment toolkit, to the task; the block ends mid-sentence and also carries the arXiv identifier (cs.CL, 30 April 2025).",
