      "target": "../sections/sec-002-related-work.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Subject indexing in library systems has evolved to\nbalance precision and adaptability, incorporating\ncontrolled vocabularies, social tagging, ontology-\nbased indexing, and hybrid approaches. Controlled\nvocabularies, such as the Library of Congress Sub-\nject Headings (LCSH), provide structured access to\nresources but require substantial intellectual effort\nto maintain consistency (Ni, 2010). While LCSH\nhas expanded through cooperative contributions, it\nfaces criticism for outdated terminology and lim-\nited flexibility (Pirmann, 2012). Social tagging,\nintroduced with Web 2.0, allows users to gen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Related work on subject indexing in library systems. Controlled vocabularies such as the Library of Congress Subject Headings (LCSH) give structured access but take much effort to keep consistent and are criticised for outdated terms and rigidity. Social tagging from Web 2.0 lets users add metadata and helps browsing, but its value in libraries is inconclusive and tags lack the specificity of controlled vocabularies. Ontology-based indexing links text to semantic concepts and improves retrieval over keyword indexing. Hybrid models in which tags supplement subject headings, as in BiblioCommons, are increasingly advocated, though catalogue interfaces still struggle to support tag-based search.",
