      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3.1\nOntoAligner Pipeline\n1) Data Representation. To align the technical\nrecords with the target subjects, we explore multi-\nple levels of information from the records for rep-\nresentation of input data: 1) Title-based Represen-\ntation: We start by using the titles of the technical\nrecords, capturing the most concise representation\nof the content. 2) Contextual Representation: We\nenhance the alignment by incorporating additional\nmetadata, such as abstracts and descriptions, pro-\nviding deeper context for each record. 3) Hierarchi-\ncal Representation: For records with hierarchical\nrelationships,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section 3.1, the OntoAligner pipeline, and the start of Section 3.2 on fine-tuning. Records can be represented by title alone, with added context (abstracts, descriptions), or with parent-level metadata. The retrieval module embeds records and subjects with a Nomic-AI embedding model and retrieves the top 30 subjects per record by cosine similarity; the LLM module, Qwen2.5-0.5B, then judges whether each candidate subject matches. After trials on the development set, the authors chose title-only input. To fine-tune the retriever they built a semantic-textual-similarity dataset of 32,952 sentence pairs, pairing titles with true subjects (score 1) and random other subjects (score 0), with at most 600 pairs per record (Table 1 gives examples), and fine-tuned nomic-embed-text-v1 with Multiple Negatives Ranking Loss for 3 epochs at batch size 32.",
