      "target": "../sections/sec-007-limitation-and-conclusion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The quantitative evaluation results in Table 2 indi-\ncate that, despite achieving a strong average recall\nof 20.30%, the model struggles with low preci-\nsion. The low precision suggests that the system re-\ntrieves a broad set of candidate subjects, but many\nare not relevant. However, this is also evident in\nqualitative results for case-2 where the precision\ndidn’t reach the same level as recall. This limita-\ntion likely stems from the small fine-tuning dataset,\nsuggesting that further fine-tuning could enhance\nperformance, particularly for smaller LLMs. Ad-\nditionally, OntoAligner’s flexibilit",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Limitations and conclusion, acknowledgments and references. Despite what the authors call a strong average recall of 20.30%, precision is low: the system retrieves many candidate subjects that are not relevant, which they attribute to the small fine-tuning dataset. They credit OntoAligner with handling embedding storage, retrieval and alignment so that users can concentrate on the LLM and retriever models, and conclude that it is effective with minimal fine-tuning but needs more data and compute to improve precision. The last author is supported by TIB and the BMBF-funded SCINEXT project (grant 01lS22070). The reference list covers LLMs4OM and OntoAligner, the LLMs4Subjects task papers, QLoRA, Sentence-BERT, Nomic Embed, Qwen2.5 and the library-tagging literature.",
